        "line": 566,
        "severity": "medium",
        "problem": "`math.atan2(d[2], pitch)` with `pitch = hypot(d[0], d[1])` is the angle between the axis and the transverse plane in 3-D (its elevation). The phantom's truth is the projection: `phantom.pedicle_axis` builds d = (side tan ped_t, -1, tan ped_s), so ped_s = atan(d_z / |d_y|), the climb's own angle in the sagittal plane; the transverse angle on the line above is likewise a projection (atan2(side d0, -d1)); the module docstring says 'the sagittal angle is the climb's angle to the transverse plane', which is atan(-rise) = atan2(d[2], -d[1]) exactly (d = -(across, 1, rise)/n); and the subtraction of atan(wall_slope), a tilt in the sagittal plane, is only exact for a sagittal-plane angle. The elevation is atan(tan s * cos t) and under-reads by a bias that grows with the transverse angle: -0.13 deg at Shapes (t 12, s 6), -1.32 at t 30 / s 10, -1.94 at t 30 / s 15, -2.62 at t 35 / s 15, before any lattice error. The old expression was the projection and was not the source of the 3 and 8 degree errors, which came from the raster-quantised middles. The height's correction is a different matter: a vertical column through the elliptical cylinder is foreshortened by cos(elevation), so `pitch = hypot(d[0], d[1])` is right for `height`; only the comment calling it 'the cosine of the sagittal angle' is wrong.",
        "input": "Exact geometry: a lumbar phantom with ped_t=30, ped_s=15 gives atan(tan 15 * cos 30) = 13.06 against 15 from the formula alone, more than TOL_DEG (1.5); Shapes (ped_t=12, ped_s=6) already loses 0.13 of its 1.5-degree budget to it, on top of the LP's own 0.4 to 0.9 degree scatter in proto3.log. (Through the pipeline the steeper phantoms P.lumbar(ped_t=30, ped_s=15) and (25, 12) are refused for other reasons I observed in 16-second runs: the left pedicle is not found and the right's columns span 5.4 mm, so a test of this point needs a phantom whose free length clears SAGITTAL_SPAN_MM at those angles.)",
        "fix": "Report the projection: `\"pedicle_angle_sagittal\": math.degrees(math.atan2(d[2], -d[1])) - math.degrees(math.atan(wall_slope(v)))`, and keep `pitch = math.hypot(d[0], d[1])` for `height` with its comment changed to say why the two differ: a vertical column crosses the pedicle on the slant of the axis's elevation, while Zindrick's sagittal angle is read on the sagittal cut. Mention the pair in the docstring sentence that defines the angle.",
