            "reason": "Confirmed on the code and on the stated input. The change is commit 0e847cd (the working tree is clean). In /home/user/bordercall/morphometry/pedicle.py, axis_from (line 486) returns d = -(across, 1, rise)/|.|, and one_side line 566 reads `math.atan2(d[2], pitch)` with `pitch = math.hypot(d[0], d[1])` (line 561). Since tan(transverse) = |across| (line 565, atan2(side*d[0], -d[1])), that is atan(-rise / sqrt(1 + across^2)) = atan(tan(s_proj) * cos(t)), the axis's elevation; the commit's own diff shows the old line was `atan2(d[2], -d[1])` = atan(-rise), the sagittal projection. The phantom's truth is the projection: morphometry/tests/phantom.py line 126 builds d = (side tan ped_t, -1, tan ped_s), so ped_s = atan(d_z/|d_y|), and line 262 sets t[\"pedicle_angle_sagittal\"] = ped_s, which Shapes.test_pedicle_angles and Straight.test_pedicles compare within TOL_DEG = 1.5 (test_morphometry.py lines 16, 72-73, 103, 120-122; Shapes is P.lumbar(ped_t=12, ped_s=6)). Feeding exact slopes through axis_from and evaluating line 566 (venv, 1 s) gives: (t 12, s 6) -> 5.87 against 6.00; (30, 10) -> 8.68; (30, 15) -> 13.06 against 15.00; (35, 15) -> 12.38; the projection expression returns 6.00/10.00/15.00/15.00 exactly, and the transverse angle returns 12/30/30/35. The bias is a pure function of the formula, before any lattice error, and grows with the transverse angle; at the tested Shapes geometry it is 0.13 degrees (within tolerance, consistent with the medium severity), at 30/15 it alone exceeds TOL_DEG. I also checked the finding's side claim about height: in phantom.py pedicle_frame, eh = (d x ez) x d = ez - (ez.d)d normalised, so a vertical column crosses the elliptic cylinder along the b semi-axis with length ped_h / hypot(d0, d1); `height = hgt * pitch` with pitch = hypot(d[0], d[1]) is therefore exactly right, and only the comment on line 561 ('the cosine of the sagittal angle') is wrong. The proposed fix (atan2(d[2], -d[1]) for the angle, keep pitch for the height, say why they differ) is sound."
