        "line": 435,
        "severity": "high",
        "problem": "The climb is the slope b of the surface of widest margin between bone and air centres (parting, lines 435-441), accepted whenever margin >= 0 and nothing lies across it (climb, lines 479-483). The slope is pinned only where the lattice cuts the true surface at several phases across the columns, which needs the surface to cross a few layer gaps over the columns' span. At 2.5 mm slices (fz 2.6 to 3, most of ENHANCE.PET per the ThickSlices docstring) a 7 mm span crosses no gap unless the surface leans more than atan(2.5/7), about 20 degrees, against the scan's axial planes. When it crosses none, every separating surface between the two layer planes is feasible and the widest-margin one is the mid-plane parallel to the lattice: b is the frame's tilt against the scan, i.e. the vertebra's inclination in the scanner (local lordosis), with a margin of half a layer and zero violations, so no rule refuses it. When it crosses exactly one gap, the widest-margin line threads the single step steeply (25 to 40 degrees). I fed parting() a synthetic 0.86 x 0.86 x 2.5 lattice tilted 0, 3 and 8 degrees about x with a planar top surface of truth 0 or 10 degrees (scratchpad thick_probe.py, interval_probe.py): the readings were 0.0 / 3.0 / 8.0 (the tilt, margin 1.25) at most phases and 27 to 38 (margin 0.25) at the rest, never near 10; the interval of slopes that still part bone from air was [-20, +20] degrees. The prototype's log covers 0.8 x 0.8 x 1 mm slices tilted 10 to 25 degrees only, where a span of 7.5 mm crosses 2 to 3 layers; the ThickSlices test checks no pedicle angle, so the suite cannot see this. The result is the measure's recurring failure: a finite, plausible, level-correlated angle (5 to 15 degrees) that is a measure of the scan's geometry.",
        "input": "Any vertebra cut at 2.5 mm whose frame lies within about 20 degrees of the axial plane (most lumbar and lower thoracic levels of a supine CT); also thin slices where the frame is nearly aligned with the scan (the prototype's least-tilted case, tilt (1,0,0) 10 turn 0, already shows margins of 0.28/0.19 against 0.00 to 0.09 elsewhere).",
        "fix": "Make the lattice say how well it pins the slope before any slope is believed: with the same constraints and margin >= 0, solve two more LPs, min b and max b (c = [0, 1, 0, 0] and [0, -1, 0, 0]); refuse the climb (NAN with a note 'the lattice leaves the pedicle's top free between <lo> and <hi> degrees') where the interval is wider than a bound calibrated on the bench (python -m morphometry bench --slice 2.5, which the ThickSlices docstring names), and carry the interval in the record for the trace. A margin cap (margin > 0.25 * fz refused) is a cheap proxy that catches the snapped readings but not the one-step 25 to 40 degree ones, whose margins are about 0.25 mm, so the interval is the right gate. Add a pedicle case to ThickSlices asserting the sagittal angle is either not read or within its stated bound, and qualify the 1.8 degree claim in the docstring and research_notes/morphometry/README.md line 68 as holding at 1 mm slices tilted 10 to 25 degrees.",
        "lens": "robustness",
        "votes": [
          {
            "refuted": false,
            "reason": "Confirmed on the real pipeline, not only on a planar probe. I ran morphometry.measure.measure_volume over the test suite's own thick-slice phantom (P.lumbar, L2-L4, spacing 0.86 x 0.86 x 2.5, grid_turn 0, tilt about x) with pedicle.parting wrapped to log each surface's slope, margin and violations (script: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/refute/thick_pipeline.py, 6-7 s a case). Results, L3 unless said: tilt 8 / truth 0 reads -8.58 (right -7.69, left -9.47) with no flag; the same volume's L2 left top and L4 both bottoms were parted at margin 1.26 (half a layer, fz 2.5 cos 8 + 0.86 sin 8), across 0, slopes -5.6 and -8.1 degrees, the layer plane's slope in the frame, exactly the snapped mid-plane the finding describes, and climb() at pedicle.py:440 and :479-483 accepts them since margin >= 0 and across == 0. Tilt 3 / truth 10 reads 17.63 (L4 right 17.83), L2's top snapped at margin 1.25. Tilt 0 / truth 10 reads 15.6 / 15.8 / 14.4. Tilt 10 / truth 10 reads 10.0 at every level with every surface at margin 1.27: the scan's tilt, right only because the surface is parallel to the layers. Tilt 20 / truth 0 (ThickSlices itself) reads -3.6 / 3.3 / -3.3, and tilt 20 / truth 10 reads 3.40 on L4 with a snapped bottom (-19.9, margin 1.33). The 1 mm control at tilt 8 / truth 0 reads 0.6 / 0.8 / -1.4, so the defect is the thick-slice lattice, as claimed. A synthetic lattice built as lattice_under selects it (refute/lattice_check.py) gives the feasible slope interval [-20, +20] degrees at 2.5 mm, with max-margin returning the tilt at margin 1.25 or a 27-38 degree thread at margin 0.25. One qualification: on the real elliptic pedicle the crown spreads the layer phases across x', so snapping hits a minority of surfaces and the one-step threads sit at 5-20 degrees rather than 25-40; the per-pedicle errors are 4-9 degrees, not the full tilt every time. That does not save the code: the readings are finite, unflagged, 3-6 times TOL_DEG, and ThickSlices asserts no pedicle angle, so the suite cannot see it; research_notes/morphometry/README.md:68 states the 1.8 degree bound with no slice-thickness qualifier. The finding's interval gate (min b, max b LPs under margin >= 0) is the right fix; a margin cap near 0.5 fz would catch only the snapped surfaces."
          },
          {
            "refuted": false,
            "reason": "Confirmed on concrete inputs through the real code path, not only the reviewer's synthetic lattice, and it matters for the measure.\n\nCode: morphometry/pedicle.py parting (line 425; the max-margin LP at 435, accepted at 440 whenever margin >= 0) and climb (451; the slope taken at 482 and averaged at 483 with no check on how well the lattice pins it). Geometrically, when the true surface crosses no scan layer over the columns' footprint, the bone centres nearest it all lie on one scan plane and the air centres on the next, and the widest-margin separator of two parallel point planes is their mid-plane, whose slope b is the layer's climb in the frame, not the pedicle's.\n\nMeasured with /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/refute_thick_pedicle.py (draws the ThickSlices phantom, 0.86 x 0.86 x 2.5 mm, column tilted about x, runs PED.one_side on L3 and the two extra LPs min b / max b; each run about 5 s):\n- tilt 8, pedicle pitched 10: top surface slope -7.4 deg = the layer's climb exactly, margin 1.260 = half a layer, on both sides: the snap the finding describes. Sagittal written 13.59 right, 16.12 left against truth 10.\n- tilt 0, level pedicle: bottom surface slope 0.6 = layer climb, margin 1.250 (reads right only because the tilt equals the truth); feasible slopes [-17.3, 18.5] deg.\n- tilt 3, level: sagittal -3.87 / -6.08 against 0; tilt 8, level: -7.69 / -9.47 against 0; tilt 0, pitched 10: 14.91 / 16.66; tilt 14, pitched 10: 0.60 (left; right not read); tilt 20 (the test's own case), level: 3.26, with feasible slopes [-20.0, 5.8].\nIn every 2.5 mm reading the interval of slopes that still part bone from air is 10 to 36 degrees wide, every margin is >= 0 with 0 across, and 9 of 10 readings taken are off by 3.6 to 9.5 degrees (TOL_DEG is 1.5), the same order as the construction this change replaced. Contrast at 1 mm: tilt 18 gives intervals 3 to 6 degrees wide and readings within about 2 degrees (1.25, -0.41 against 0; 8.45, 8.05 against 10); tilt 3 at 1 mm gives 0.05, 1.78 and 11.01, 13.47, intervals 3 to 5 wide. So the proposed gate on the interval's width would discriminate, and the 1.8 degree claim (docstring; research_notes/morphometry/README.md line 68) holds only for thin slices.\n\nWhy it matters: pedicle_angle_sagittal is written finite and unflagged (one_side line 566), feeds the height's pitch correction (561, 563) and the chord's axis, on the slice thickness most of ENHANCE.PET is cut at, and ThickSlices (test_morphometry.py 216) asserts nothing on it, so the suite cannot see it.\n\nTwo qualifications to the finding's wording: on the real path the one-step case did not give the 25 to 40 degree readings its synthetic probe did, but picks 4 to 10 degrees off inside the wide interval, so the number is not always the vertebra's tilt itself; the harm is the same, an unpinned slope believed. Also seen in passing, outside this finding: wall_slope corrects the straight phantom's sagittal by -4.5 (tilt 20) and -2.4 (tilt 3) degrees on 2.5 mm slices, and the left pedicle spanned 0.9 to 1.7 mm and was not read at tilts 0 and 20."
