            "reason": "Not refuted: the behaviour is as claimed and it gives a silent wrong number on the project's commonest input. pedicle.py:483 returns 0.5 * (slopes[0] + slopes[1]) with no comparison of the two; the only gates before it are per surface (line 472 MIN_PARTED, 476 parting None, 479 VIOLATED_SHARE), and all pass whenever each surface is parted with margin > 0 and 0 across.\n\nConcrete input, through the real path (one_side, via scratchpad/refute_thick_pedicle.py, each run under 5 s): the suite's own ThickSlices geometry family, the straight phantom (truth sagittal 0, TOL_DEG 1.5) drawn at 0.86 x 0.86 x 2.5 mm, \"as most of ENHANCE.PET's CTs are cut\" (test_morphometry.py, class ThickSlices), tilted about x. tilt 8: left top 5.1 deg, bottom 11.5 deg (6.4 apart), both margin > 0 and 0 across, sagittal reported -9.47 against 0, no flag; right 5.1/8.0, -7.69. tilt 3: 7.5/5.1 -> -3.87; 10.0/7.1 -> -6.08. tilt 12: 4.2/7.5 -> -6.05; 6.4/4.3 -> -5.53. tilt 20 (ThickSlices itself): right 2.5/-0.0 -> 3.26. Every one is a silent error of 3 to 9.5 degrees against a 1.5 degree tolerance, and ThickSlices asserts nothing on pedicle angles, so the suite cannot see it. The synthetic parting()-only probe (scratchpad/thick_probe.py) also confirms the \"0.0 then 22.6\" claim: 2.5 mm slices, tilt 0, truth 10 reads 30.2, 22.6, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, 22.6 over six phases, margins 0.18 to 1.25, 0 across. At 1 mm (the suite's geometry, proto3.log \"LP near\") the two surfaces agree within about a degree and the readings are within 1.8 of truth, so the defect is confined to thick slices.\n\nThree corrections to the finding as written. (1) Its proto3.log evidence is a misreading: the \"m\" column is each surface's margin in mm (proto3_sagittal.py line 147, margins.append(w[4])), not its slope; \"0.04/-1.76\" is a top margin of 0.04 and a bottom margin of -1.76, a bottom not parted at all, which is the case line 479 refuses, and the \"taken\" rows are not used by the code. (2) Through the real path the surfaces disagree by 2 to 6 degrees, not 25, while the average errs by 3 to 9.5: both surfaces err the same way because each max-margin solution sits near one end of a 16 to 26 degree feasible interval (at tilt 8: top [-7.5, 8.7], bottom [-7.5, 12.5]; at tilt 20: [-20.0, 5.8] and [-19.9, 2.6]). So an agreement bound of a few degrees flags the worst sample (tilt 8 left, 6.4 apart) but passes others equally wrong (tilt 12 left, 2.1 apart, 5.5 off; tilt 3 right, 2.4 apart, 3.9 off). It is a cheap partial guard; the fix that reaches the cause is per surface, refusing (or flagging) where the interval of slopes that still part bone from air is wider than a bound, or taking that interval's middle in place of the max-margin vertex, which is finding 1. (3) Both slopes are already kept: record[\"top\"][\"coefficients\"][1] and record[\"bottom\"][\"coefficients\"][1] (pedicle.py lines 478 and 482 through parted in one_side's info), and trace_pedicle.py lines 75 to 79 print each surface's margin, across and slope side by side, so that part of the proposed fix is done.\n\nVerdict for the lens: it matters for the measure, not cosmetically: a silent sagittal angle 3 to 9.5 degrees wrong on 2.5 mm CTs, with the two surfaces' disagreement one visible symptom of it. Medium is a fair severity; the agreement check is worth adding as a flag (naming both slopes in degrees, as proposed), with the interval bound of finding 1 as the real remedy."
