            "reason": "Confirmed in the code and by measurement; the finding stands as a real definitional defect (medium), with one qualification on its practical size.\n\nWhat the code does. /home/user/bordercall/morphometry/pedicle.py:561 sets pitch = hypot(d[0], d[1]) and :566 computes pedicle_angle_sagittal = degrees(atan2(d[2], pitch)) - wall term: the axis's elevation above the transverse plane. The saved pre-change copy (scratchpad pedicle.py.before, diff against the current file) had atan2(d[2], -d[1]), the slope in the sagittal projection, and pitch = abs(d[1])/hypot(d[1], d[2]). The LP's climb is the projection slope: lattice_under (pedicle.py:421) defines x' as side*(x - midline) - (polyval(line, y) + sec/2), the offset from a centre line that follows the wall line in xy, so at fixed x' a point translates along a cylinder's axis and dz/dy of the surface is exactly -tan(ped_s); axis_from (:497) keeps that as d's dz/dy, so atan2(d[2], -d[1]) returns ped_s and atan2(d[2], hypot(d[0], d[1])) returns atan(tan ped_s * cos ped_t).\n\nWhat the truth is. morphometry/tests/phantom.py:126 builds the axis as (side*tan ped_t, -1, tan ped_s) and :262 sets truth[\"pedicle_angle_sagittal\"] = ped_s, i.e. the sagittal projection. The repository's paper definitions agree: the sagittal angle is read on a lateral image or a sagittal CT plane (research_notes/clinical-reread/notes/lien2007.md:146), and morphometry/__main__.py:36 aliases Panjabi's pedicle_incl_trans to this key for compare.\n\nArithmetic (venv, pure math on the axis_from vector): gap projection - elevation = 0.13 deg at (s 6, t 12), 1.10 at (12, 25), 1.32 at (10, 30), 1.94 at (15, 30), 0 when either angle is 0. The suite's only pitched phantom is Shapes at ped_t 12, ped_s 6 (test_morphometry.py:103), where 0.13 is far inside TOL_DEG 1.5, so the suite cannot tell the two definitions apart.\n\nMeasured on the pipeline (refute_steep_kw.py in the scratchpad, a variant of the reviewer's review_steep.py taking phantom fields, one straight lumbar phantom at the suite's 7 degree grid turn, 16 to 17 s each): with the pillar moved back and out so the slanted pedicle stays free (span 5.8 mm, 60 columns), at ped_t 30 / ped_s 15 the code reads 12.41 and 13.79 (mean 13.10, exact elevation 13.06) while the projection of the same axis reads 14.30 and 15.67 (mean 14.99) against truth 15.00; at ped_t 25 / ped_s 12 the code reads 11.93 and 10.56 (mean 11.25, exact elevation 10.90) while the projection reads 13.13 and 11.55 (mean 12.34) against truth 12.00. The transverse angle (30.54/29.14; 25.18/24.22) and the height (15.00/14.83; 14.74/14.15 against 15) are right, so the height's cosine at :561 is correct as the reviewer says; only the angle's denominator is wrong against the phantom's own truth.\n\nQualifications that do not refute it: the suite's default phantom at ped_t 30 does not read the sagittal angle at all (free pedicle 4.7 mm < SAGITTAL_SPAN_MM 5.5), so the stock phantom cannot show it; the prototype's \"LP near\" column (proto3_sagittal.py:128-149) also used atan(b * cos t), the elevation, so its validation never covered the difference; and the papers' lumbar sagittal angles are mostly under 5 degrees, where the bias at an L5's 30 degree transverse angle is about 0.3 to 0.7 degrees rather than 1.3 to 1.9, which is why I would keep it at medium rather than high. Fix as proposed: keep pitch for pedicle_h and write the angle as math.degrees(math.atan2(d[2], -d[1])) - the wall term, and add a Shapes-like case with ped_t of 25 or more and a longer free pedicle (ped_back 8, pillar at pil_y -20) so the test can tell the two apart."
          },
