M or more of rows no affine function of (y, x', x'^2) strictly separates the interleaved classes; the 20 prototype readings in /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/proto3.log carry 173 to 279 centres per class with margins of 0.00 to 0.09 mm and none unbounded. So the wrong note is reachable by a direct call to parting or by a numerical failure, not by a scan's lattice. The proposed fix is still cheap and right: test res.status == 3 at line 437 and give climb a note that says the centres do not bracket the surface, keeping the present wording for the other statuses; bounding the slopes would also work but changes the program for every reading, so the status test is the smaller change.",
          "The behaviour is as described but it does not touch the measure. Confirmed on the finding's input with the repository's venv (scipy 1.18.1): parting(y=[-12]*6+[-5]*6, across=0, z=0, bone=[T]*6+[F]*6, top=True) returns None, HiGHS status 3 \"The problem is unbounded\"; an all-bone input and a parabola-separable one-centre-per-column input do the same. In climb (morphometry/pedicle.py:476-477) that None gives rise=NAN with the note \"no surface parts the bone from the air about the pedicle's top\". Downstream (pedicle.py:556-570) axis_from takes the NaN rise as level and reads \"transverse\"; one_side then sets pedicle_angle_sagittal = NAN, applies no pitch correction to the height, and carries the note out as `note = why`. So there is no wrong number, no crash, and no silent miss: the reading is withheld and recorded, and only the sentence's reason is wrong (every surface of the family parts them, rather than none). That is cosmetic for the measure; its weight is in the trace alone. Two further points against treating it as a defect: (1) the realistic trigger the finding names cannot occur. SURFACE_LAYERS = 1.5 (pedicle.py:140) and G.surface (scanimport/geometry.py:275-279) puts the column's surface at most 0.5 fz beyond the outermost bone centre, so under a grid aligned with the frame every column's window holds that bone centre and the first air centre above it (at most fz beyond the surface; a centre beyond the volume is enumerated by lattice_under and counts as air) at the same (y, x'), which makes strict separation in (y, x', x'^2) impossible and the margin program bounded (verified: a bracketed stack gives margin 0.5, across 0). The unbounded case needs bone and air footprints disjoint in (y, x', x'^2), i.e. a handful of centres with no column holding both, which MIN_PARTED = 4 only loosely guards against and which is rare in a scan. (2) In that case the surface's z is undetermined by the data, so withholding the reading is the right outcome; the proposed alternative of bounding |b|, |c|, |d| would return an arbitrary surface with positive margin and across = 0 that the gates would accept, giving an unfounded climb, which is worse than the present behaviour. The only sound change is to tell status 3 (and 4) apart in the note for the trace's sake, a wording improvement and not a correctness fix."
