      {
        "title": "The count of centres across the surface uses 1e-9 against HiGHS's 1e-7 feasibility tolerance",
        "file": "/home/user/bordercall/morphometry/pedicle.py",
        "line": 448,
        "lens": "geometry",
        "reasons": [
          "The claimed input (a crossing xi of about 3e-8 on a centre the L1-optimal surface passes through, counted by `crossing > 1e-9` at /home/user/bordercall/morphometry/pedicle.py:448) does not occur. The 1e-7 figure is HiGHS's accepted bound violation, not the error in a returned value. scipy 1.18.1's `method=\"highs\"` runs the dual simplex on this program (its result equals `highs-ds` bit for bit on 40 of 40 problems and never `highs-ipm`), so the solution is a vertex: a nonbasic xi is exactly 0.0, and the centres the surface passes through come back as exact zeros (e.g. a 70-centre fallback: 4 centres on the surface, 65 xi exactly 0.0, smallest positive xi 0.71 mm). A degenerate basic xi carries only basis-solve round-off: running pedicle.parting itself and the same crossing program over about 7,300 random lattice problems (about 6,750 reaching the fallback, about 700 of them on sets of 10 centres or fewer, with frame coordinates offset to 60 mm, x'^2 to 100, columns crowded within 0.3 mm in y, slices to 5 mm), the largest xi in (0, 1e-4) was 6.0e-12, three orders below 1e-9, and the count at 1e-9 never differed from the count at 1e-6 (0 of about 6,750). Even the IPM path, which crosses over to a basis by default, returned no xi in (1e-9, 1e-6). The second half of the proposed fix guards a case that did not occur either: no margin fell in (-1e-6, 0) in any trial, and had one, the fallback's crossing program returns exact zeros for a parted set, so across = 0 and the surface is still read. The 1e-9 count is therefore a correct filter for the solver's actual noise and overstates `across` on no concrete input found."
