        "lens": "robustness",
        "reasons": [
          "The mechanism inside parting() is real in isolation: fed a set whose bone centres all lie in front of its air centres (4 bone at y 0..1.5, 4 air at y 4..5.5), the first linprog at /home/user/bordercall/morphometry/pedicle.py:435 ends with HiGHS status 3 (\"The problem is unbounded\"), res.success is False, parting returns None at line 438, and climb at line 468 would write \"no surface parts the bone from the air about the pedicle's top\". But the claimed pedicle input, a band holding bone only under the front rows and air only under the back ones, is not one lattice_under (lines 398-423) can produce, so the note is never reached by this path. Each column's band is centred on that column's own top, read by height_run over a window (0.75 pixel) that contains the column's cell (0.5 pixel across, 0.25 along y); so every grid column through the cell has its top bone centre within fz*cos(tilt) of the window's maximum, which is within 1.5 fz of zs, and its next centre up, air, at most 0.5 fz above zs: both are kept. The cells of neighbouring columns tile the region (rows half a pixel apart, columns a pixel apart), so the kept set is a dense strip of the tilted lattice, about three layers deep over the whole region, not one or two centres per column, whatever the slice thickness; the count scales with the region's area over fx*fy, not with fz. In an untilted frame the bone centre and the air centre above it share (y, x') exactly, which bounds t by half their z gap; in a tilted frame they are offset by the same small vector at every grid column across a span of at least SAGITTAL_SPAN_MM (5.5 mm, several lattice periods), an alternation no surface affine in y and quadratic in x' can separate, so the margin stays bounded. Probes through the real pipeline with the linprog statuses recorded (default lumbar at 0.5x0.5x2.5 mm under a 12 degree pitch: 132 columns, 486 and 520 centres, 128 and 127 columns holding both bone and air, 0 bone-only; a 6 mm wide, 7 mm high pedicle at 0.5x0.5x2.5 mm under an 18 degree compound tilt and a 7 degree turn: 36 columns, 252 and 258 centres, 35 and 36 columns with both, 0 bone-only) all returned status 0 with margins 0.02-0.05 mm. Capping t would be harmless, but it addresses a configuration the band's construction excludes.",
          "The mechanism exists in the abstract but the input is not reachable from the code's own band, and even if it were the effect on the measure is nil.\n\n1. Mechanism (confirmed, not disputed): /home/user/bordercall/morphometry/pedicle.py:435-437 maximises t with every variable free; on a toy set where bone and air are separable in (y, x') alone HiGHS returns status 3, success False, parting returns None, and climb (line 476) writes \"no surface parts the bone from the air about the pedicle's top\".\n\n2. Reachability (refuted): lattice_under (lines 398-422) keeps the centres within SURFACE_LAYERS = 1.5 layers of each column's own surface, and that surface comes from height_run (lines 212-231) as the highest bone centre in a 1.5 px box plus half a layer, so it lies within half a layer of the true top at that place. Hence every lattice line that crosses the band contributes the bone centre just below the top and the air centre just above it, both kept (their distance to the owning column's surface is at most 1.5 fz by construction), and the two differ in (y, x') by at most fz*tan(tilt) per layer. For the finding's input, bone only under the front rows and air only under the back, every lattice line would have to be an edge line, which needs fz*tan(tilt) >= half the span, and climb already refuses a span under SAGITTAL_SPAN_MM = 5.5 mm (line 464): at 2.5 mm slices that is a tilt of 48 degrees or more between frame and scan, beyond anything the docstring reports (30 degrees at most). A computation building the band exactly as lattice_under builds it (/tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/lp_bound_check.py) over a 6 x 3 mm region with px 0.6, fz 2.5, pitches 0, 5, 10, 20, 30, 45, 50 and 60 degrees, surface slopes 0 and 15 degrees, and with a 20 degree roll added, at both slice thicknesses, gave LP status 0 (bounded, optimal) in every case; the smallest margin was 0.14 mm, never unbounded.\n\n3. Lens, does it matter: no. Where parting returns None the sagittal angle is NAN and the side is flagged either way (one_side, lines 557-558: note = why when reads == \"transverse\"; measures writes it to flags[\"pedicle_<side>\"]). No wrong number, no crash, no silent miss; only the wording of a note on a reading that is correctly withheld would differ. That is cosmetic, as the finding's own severity (low) and its last sentence concede.\n\n4. The proposed fix is worse on its own: with t capped, a separable band returns margin = cap >= 0 and across = 0, so climb (line 481) takes b from whichever vertex HiGHS stops at; on the toy band the cap gave b = -2.0 and -7.5 (climbs of -63 and -82 degrees), any larger b being equally optimal. The withheld reading would become an arbitrary number unless a separate margin gate (the finding defers to \"finding 1\") refuses it, so the fix depends on another change and introduces the very wrong-number risk the current None avoids. Naming the LP status in the note is a wording improvement only."
