        "line": 479,
        "lens": "robustness",
        "reasons": [
          "The comparison at /home/user/bordercall/morphometry/pedicle.py:479 is as described (18 across of 200 would pass 0.1 * 200), but no band that lattice_under builds has the 180-bone/20-air composition the finding needs, and the named input moves the band the other way.\n\nConstruction (pedicle.py:398-422 and 212-233): each centre's band is |z - zs[j]| <= 1.5 fz about its own column's surface, and that surface comes from height_run over a window (0.75 px in x and y) that contains the band's window (0.5 px in x, 0.25 px in y), read from the same mask. The run through the row's z over a superset of voxels tops out no lower than the band column's own bone, so the estimate can only stand at or above the band column's top (at or below its bottom for the bottom surface). An estimate \"a layer too high\" therefore skews a top band into the air, not into the bone, and by at most two centres in that column; a staircase or a segmenter's overshoot is in both readings alike, since both read the same voxels. A 9:1 skew toward bone cannot come out of this code.\n\nMeasurement (script /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/refute2/band_balance.py, which runs one_side and tallies the returned bands per column): the shapes phantom at the 25-degree tilt with turn 15 gave top/bottom bands of 108/139, 108/139, 112/130, 106/126 (bone/air; minority share 0.44-0.46); the finding's own case, a 10-degree pitched pedicle on 2.5 mm axis-aligned slices (the partial-volume staircase), gave 120/135, 120/133, 108/117, 109/122 (minority share 0.47-0.48), with per-column counts mostly (2,2) and the skewed columns going both ways ((1,0),(2,0) as well as (0,1),(0,3)). The committed traces agree (trace-straight.log and trace-shapes.log: 103/132, 96/124, 90/112, 97/124, 99/116, 90/107, 110/161, 111/154). At that balance VIOLATED_SHARE * len(q) is 21-25 percent of the minority class, so even a surface at the threshold still parts more than three quarters of the minority; \"parts nothing\" is not reached on any input that reaches line 479. Counting the share per class would be a harmless hardening, not the repair of a defect.",
          "The skew the finding needs cannot arise from the construction, and the direction it names runs the other way. In morphometry/pedicle.py, lattice_under (lines 408-432) centres each column's band on that column's own surface estimate zs = cols[:,3] (top) or cols[:,2] (bottom), which columns() (line 395) read with height_run (lines 215-233): the run's top is the highest bone centre within 0.75 pixel of the column, a neighbourhood that contains the band's own stacks (assigned within 0.5 pixel across and 0.25 pixel front to back, lines 423-426), and G.surface puts the estimate half a gap beyond it. So for the top band every bone centre lies at or below S - 0.5*gap and the air starts right above: the band holds at most 1.5 fz of bone depth and at least 1.5 fz of air (and the bottom band mirrors this). The estimate can only ever be too high for the band's stacks (a neighbour a staircase step up), which moves the top band up into the AIR, not into the bone as the finding's input says; the bone-heavy 180/20 band is unreachable, and so is 'nearly every air centre on the wrong side'. Measured on the prototype's phantoms through PED.one_side (record['top'/'bottom'] bone/air/across): 1 mm slices, column tilted 18 degrees: bone share 0.38-0.45, across 0; shapes at the 25-degree tilt: 0.44-0.46, margin -0.011, across 2 of 247 (both classes); 3 mm slices aligned: 126/126; 3 mm slices tilted 18 degrees (the finding's thick-slice case): bone share 0.26-0.31, margin positive, across 0. The worst skew seen is thus about 1:3 and toward air, where 10 per cent of all is 30-40 per cent of the minority; but there the margin is positive and the least-crossing stage never runs, and line 479 is only a guard after the LP already minimised the total crossing, so it changes no number on any input I could produce. With no concrete input on which a surface that parts nothing passes the check, the finding stands as a hypothetical; the min(n_bone, n_air) denominator would be a harmless tightening, not a fix to a defect."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "The crossing threshold of 1e-9 counts solver noise as centres across the surface",
        "file": "/home/user/bordercall/morphometry/pedicle.py",
        "line": 448,
        "lens": "robustness",
        "reasons": [
          "The slacks at /home/user/bordercall/morphometry/pedicle.py:447-448 come from a basic solution: scipy's method=\"highs\" on an LP of this size runs HiGHS's dual simplex (and HiGHS's IPM path runs crossover by default), so a slack s_i is either nonbasic at exactly 0 or basic with a value solved from the basis, whose error is roundoff on these magnitudes, not the 1e-7 primal feasibility tolerance, which is HiGHS's acceptance tolerance for constraint violation and not a noise floor on returned values. Measured on concrete input (script /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/refute/slack_noise.py, calling the real parting() on 398 synthetic lattice bands shaped like lattice_under's output: rows half a pixel apart, columns a pixel apart, layers fz apart, frame coordinates of tens of mm, labels from a quadric with flips so the L1 program runs, and the boundary often placed exactly on a lattice layer so whole layers of centres lie on the least-crossing surface): 18,838 centres lay exactly on the surface (|residual| < 1e-6), the largest slack on any of them was 2.5e-12, every slack in (0, 1e-6] was at most 2.5e-12 (504 of them, all roundoff), and the largest |slack - max(0, residual)| over every centre of every solve was 2.6e-11, four orders below the 1e-9 threshold. No centre was counted across by solver noise, so neither the trace nor the VIOLATED_SHARE test is affected. The proposed fix would itself miscount: in 3 of the 398 cases a 1e-3 mm cut dropped centres that genuinely lie 1.2e-4 to 9.9e-4 mm across the surface (their residuals, not noise), so it is the length threshold, not the 1e-9 one, that would misreport.",
          "The premise does not hold on a concrete computation, and even if it did the effect would be loud and bounded, not a wrong number.\n\nWhat the code does (/home/user/bordercall/morphometry/pedicle.py, parting, lines 430-448): the second program minimises sum(s_i) subject to sign_i*(F_i coef) - s_i <= sign_i*z_i, s_i >= 0, with scipy's method=\"highs\". scipy's HiGHS path returns a basic (vertex) solution from the simplex, not an interior point: a slack that is nonbasic sits at exactly 0.0, and a basic one that happens to be zero carries only the round-off of the basis solve. HiGHS's 1e-7 primal feasibility tolerance bounds how far a constraint may be violated, which can only make s_i slightly smaller than the true crossing (an undercount of a centre across by less than 1e-7 mm), never put 1e-8 on a centre that lies on the surface.\n\nComputation (scripts at /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/refute/slack_noise.py and slack_sizes.py, run with the scratchpad venv): 84 bands built the way lattice_under builds them (fz 1.24 or 0.8 mm, pixel 0.6 or 0.4 mm, axis-aligned and tilted 3 to 12 degrees, frame offsets up to 42 mm, labels flipped so the margin program fails and the L1 program runs, n from 36 to 3600), each solved with parting's exact second program. In every one of the 84 solves: zero slacks fell in the noise band (1e-12, 1e-6]; the largest slack on any centre lying on the surface (|residual| <= 1e-6, up to 48 such centres per band in the degenerate axis-aligned cases) was 8e-14 mm; \"across\" counted with 1e-9 equalled the count with 1e-3 except in two bands where the 1e-3 threshold dropped two centres that genuinely cross by between 1e-9 and 1e-3 mm, i.e. the proposed fix would undercount, the current code does not overcount. So the input the finding names, \"several centres lying exactly on the least-crossing surface\", is exercised (4 to 48 per band) and produces no spurious count.\n\nLens: even granting the premise, `across` reaches only two places: the trace's print (morphometry/tests/trace_pedicle.py:78) and the test `got[\"across\"] > VIOLATED_SHARE * len(q)` at pedicle.py:479, which runs only when the margin program has already failed (margin < 0). A handful of extra counts could at most tip a band sitting within that handful of 10 per cent of len(q) from read to not read, and the result of that tip is NAN with a note that names the counts, a loud refusal, never a wrong angle, a crash or a silent miss. No change is warranted; replacing 1e-9 with a length threshold would only hide real sub-micron crossings."
        ]
      },
